---

<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="50–60 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Phrasal verbs: <em>store meaning and pattern together</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">A multiword verb is useful only when you know what the whole chunk means in context, whether it needs an object, where that object goes, and how the verb changes for time. Learn <span class="ex">turn something off</span>, <span class="ex">look for something</span>, and <span class="ex">show up</span> as different patterns, not as one rule about a small word.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">50–60 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">meaning · tense · object pattern</span>
    <span class="tag">turn it off · look for it · show up</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Match six chunks to six jobs in one handoff.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Separate no-object, movable-object, and fixed patterns.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Choose meaning, tense, object position, and register.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Give a fictional workshop handoff and clarify meaning.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can treat a common multiword verb as one meaning unit; change its main verb for tense while keeping the particle stable; distinguish chunks used without an object, separable chunks, and chunks that stay together; place noun and pronoun objects accurately; use context to select among multiple meanings; choose a neutral or more official alternative when needed; hear stress and linking; and clarify an unfamiliar meaning.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved past simple, the base verb after did, and an imperative with an object pronoun.">
    <h3>Three earlier patterns</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use past simple for yesterday, the base verb after <span class="ex">did</span>, and the base verb at the start of an imperative.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier pattern choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="picked">picked</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="call">call</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Write">Write</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="pick">pick</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="called">called</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Wrote">Wrote</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The collection happened yesterday: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="picked" data-hint="Use the regular past form before up for a finished collection yesterday." data-fix="Maya picked the supply boxes up yesterday.">choose</button> the supply boxes up yesterday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="ex">Did</span> already carries past time: Did Leo <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="call" data-hint="Use the base verb after did; the particle back does not change." data-fix="Did Leo call the coordinator back?">choose</button> the coordinator back?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The direct instruction begins with the base verb: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Write" data-hint="Begin an affirmative imperative with the base verb Write." data-fix="Write the room number down.">choose</button> the room number down.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice six chunks doing six different jobs</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Harbor workshop handoff</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">“Please <strong>pick up</strong> the supply box at the front desk and <strong>write down</strong> room 204. <strong>Look for</strong> the blue sign-in sheets in the cabinet, then <strong>turn on</strong> the projector. The coordinator left a message, so <strong>call her back</strong>. After the workshop, <strong>throw away</strong> only the unused blank paper.”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which chunk means collect? Which one means record information?</li>
      <li>Which chunks mean search, activate, return a call, and discard?</li>
      <li>Which chunks have an object between their two parts? Which one stays together before its object?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched pick up, write down, look for, turn on, call back, and throw away to six distinct handoff jobs.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify the job of each chunk</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Phrasal-verb job choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="collect an item">collect an item</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="record information">record information</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="search for something">search for something</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="activate equipment">activate equipment</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="return a phone call">return a phone call</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="discard something">discard something</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span><span class="ex">Pick up the supply box at the front desk</span> means <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="collect an item" data-hint="The box is waiting at a named place for Maya to receive it." data-fix="Pick up the supply box means collect an item.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="ex">Write down room 204</span> means <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="record information" data-hint="The room number should be written so it is not forgotten." data-fix="Write down room 204 means record information.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><span class="ex">Look for the blue sheets</span> means <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="search for something" data-hint="The sheets are not yet located, so the person tries to find them." data-fix="Look for the blue sheets means search for something.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">Turn on the projector</span> means <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="activate equipment" data-hint="The projector is off and needs to start operating." data-fix="Turn on the projector means activate equipment.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span><span class="ex">Call the coordinator back</span> means <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="return a phone call" data-hint="The coordinator called first and left a message." data-fix="Call the coordinator back means return a phone call.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span><span class="ex">Throw away the unused paper</span> means <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="discard something" data-hint="Only blank paper no longer needed goes into the correct waste container." data-fix="Throw away the unused paper means discard something.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: store four facts, not two words</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>For each multiword verb, store the whole chunk, its meaning in one context, its object pattern, and a complete example. A particle such as <span class="ex">up, off, out</span>, or <span class="ex">back</span> can show a physical direction, a result, or a meaning that is difficult to predict.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>chunk</th><th>meaning in this context</th><th>object pattern</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">show up</span></td><td>arrive</td><td>used without an object here</td><td><span class="ex">Maya showed up at 5:30.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">turn off</span></td><td>stop equipment operating</td><td>separable</td><td><span class="ex">Turn the projector off. Turn it off.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">look for</span></td><td>try to find</td><td>stays together before the object</td><td><span class="ex">Look for the sheets. Look for them.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">take off</span></td><td>remove clothing or leave the ground</td><td>pattern changes with meaning</td><td><span class="ex">Take your coat off. The plane took off.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The main verb carries tense and agreement; the particle stays the same: <span class="ex">call back · calls back · called back · is calling back</span>. After <span class="ex">did / didn’t</span>, return the main verb to its base form: <span class="ex">Did she call back? She didn’t call back.</span></p>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>A practical label, not a false grammar rule</h3>
    <p>This lesson uses <span class="ex">phrasal verb</span> as a learner-friendly label for common verb-particle and fixed verb-preposition chunks. Some grammar books label combinations such as <span class="ex">look for</span> or <span class="ex">wait for</span> differently. The useful A2 question is the same: What does the whole chunk mean here, and where does its object go?</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Pattern 1: no object in the meaning used here</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>These chunks form a complete idea without a direct object in the meanings below. A following time, place, or manner phrase is not a movable object: <span class="ex">showed up at 5:30 · came back after lunch</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>base chunk</th><th>past form</th><th>meaning used here</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody><tr><td><span class="ex">get up</span></td><td><span class="ex">got up</span></td><td>leave bed</td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">go out</span></td><td><span class="ex">went out</span></td><td>leave home, often socially</td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">come back</span></td><td><span class="ex">came back</span></td><td>return</td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">sit down</span></td><td><span class="ex">sat down</span></td><td>move into a seated position</td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">show up</span></td><td><span class="ex">showed up</span></td><td>arrive, often when expected</td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">grow up</span></td><td><span class="ex">grew up</span></td><td>develop from childhood to adulthood</td></tr></tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You changed the main verb for six finished events while keeping up, out, back, and down stable.">
    <h3>Drill 1: choose the finished no-object chunk</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="No-object chunk choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="got up">got up</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="went out">went out</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="came back">came back</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="sat down">sat down</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="showed up">showed up</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="grew up">grew up</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Leo left his bed at 6:30 yesterday: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="got up" data-hint="Use the irregular past of get plus the stable particle up." data-fix="Leo got up at 6:30 yesterday.">choose</button> at 6:30.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Maya left home for dinner with friends on Friday: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="went out" data-hint="Use the irregular past of go for the social meaning of go out." data-fix="Maya went out for dinner on Friday.">choose</button> for dinner.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Leo returned from lunch at 1:00: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="came back" data-hint="Use the irregular past of come plus back to report a return." data-fix="Leo came back from lunch at 1:00.">choose</button> at 1:00.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The chair was empty, and Maya moved into a seated position: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="sat down" data-hint="Use the irregular past of sit plus down." data-fix="Maya sat down in the empty chair.">choose</button> in the chair.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The workshop expected Leo at 5:30, and he arrived exactly then: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="showed up" data-hint="Show up means arrive, often at an expected event." data-fix="Leo showed up at 5:30.">choose</button> at 5:30.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Maya spent her childhood in Phoenix: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="grew up" data-hint="Use the irregular past of grow plus up for childhood development." data-fix="Maya grew up in Phoenix.">choose</button> in Phoenix.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Pattern 2: a separable chunk has two noun positions</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>With these meanings, a short noun can follow the particle or sit between the verb and particle: <span class="ex">turn off the projector · turn the projector off</span>. A personal pronoun must go in the middle: <span class="ex">turn it off</span>, not <span class="ex">turn off it</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>chunk and meaning</th><th>noun object</th><th>pronoun object</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody><tr><td><span class="ex">turn on</span>: activate</td><td><span class="ex">turn on the projector · turn the projector on</span></td><td><span class="ex">turn it on</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">pick up</span>: collect</td><td><span class="ex">pick up the boxes · pick the boxes up</span></td><td><span class="ex">pick them up</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">write down</span>: record</td><td><span class="ex">write down the number · write the number down</span></td><td><span class="ex">write it down</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">fill out</span>: complete a form</td><td><span class="ex">fill out the form · fill the form out</span></td><td><span class="ex">fill it out</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">throw away</span>: discard</td><td><span class="ex">throw away the paper · throw the paper away</span></td><td><span class="ex">throw it away</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">call back</span>: return a call</td><td><span class="ex">call the coordinator back</span></td><td><span class="ex">call her back</span></td></tr></tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You placed six pronoun objects between the main verb and particle in separable chunks.">
    <h3>Drill 2: place the pronoun inside the chunk</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Separable chunk choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="turned it on">turned it on</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="picked them up">picked them up</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="wrote it down">wrote it down</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="filled it out">filled it out</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="threw them away">threw them away</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="called her back">called her back</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The projector was off; <span class="ex">it</span> replaces the projector: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="turned it on" data-hint="Put it between turned and on." data-fix="Maya turned it on.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Two boxes were waiting; <span class="ex">them</span> replaces the boxes: Leo <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="picked them up" data-hint="Put them between picked and up." data-fix="Leo picked them up at the front desk.">choose</button> at the front desk.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Room 204 was easy to forget; <span class="ex">it</span> replaces the room number: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="wrote it down" data-hint="Put it between wrote and down." data-fix="Maya wrote it down.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The fictional form was complete; <span class="ex">it</span> replaces the form: Leo <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="filled it out" data-hint="Put it between filled and out." data-fix="Leo filled it out.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The two unused blank sheets were no longer needed; <span class="ex">them</span> replaces the sheets: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="threw them away" data-hint="Put them between threw and away." data-fix="Maya threw them away in the paper-recycling bin.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The coordinator called first; <span class="ex">her</span> replaces the coordinator: Leo <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="called her back" data-hint="Put her between called and back." data-fix="Leo called her back.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Pattern 3: keep the chunk together before its object</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Other common combinations keep the verb and following preposition or particle together. The noun or pronoun comes after the whole chunk: <span class="ex">look for the sheets · look for them</span>, not <span class="ex">look them for</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>chunk</th><th>meaning used here</th><th>noun and pronoun pattern</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody><tr><td><span class="ex">look for</span></td><td>try to find</td><td><span class="ex">look for the sheets · look for them</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">wait for</span></td><td>stay until someone or something arrives</td><td><span class="ex">wait for Maya · wait for her</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">listen to</span></td><td>pay attention to sound</td><td><span class="ex">listen to the message · listen to it</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">look after</span></td><td>take care of</td><td><span class="ex">look after the guest · look after him</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">get on</span></td><td>board a bus, train, or similar vehicle</td><td><span class="ex">get on the bus · get on it</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">get off</span></td><td>leave a bus, train, or similar vehicle</td><td><span class="ex">get off the bus · get off it</span></td></tr></tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You kept look for, wait for, listen to, look after, get on, and get off together before their pronoun objects.">
    <h3>Drill 3: keep the fixed chunk together</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Fixed chunk choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="looked for them">looked for them</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="waited for her">waited for her</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="listened to it">listened to it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="looked after him">looked after him</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="got on it">got on it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="got off it">got off it</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The blue sheets were missing, and <span class="ex">them</span> replaces the sheets: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="looked for them" data-hint="Keep looked for together before them." data-fix="Maya looked for them in the cabinet.">choose</button> in the cabinet.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Leo stayed at the entrance until Maya arrived; <span class="ex">her</span> replaces Maya: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="waited for her" data-hint="Keep waited for together before her." data-fix="Leo waited for her at the entrance.">choose</button> at the entrance.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The coordinator’s message contained the room change; <span class="ex">it</span> replaces the message: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="listened to it" data-hint="Keep listened to together before it." data-fix="Maya listened to it twice.">choose</button> twice.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>A fictional guest needed help finding the room; <span class="ex">him</span> replaces the guest: Leo <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="looked after him" data-hint="Keep looked after together before him." data-fix="Leo looked after him until the coordinator arrived.">choose</button> until the coordinator arrived.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The Harbor shuttle arrived, and <span class="ex">it</span> replaces the shuttle: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="got on it" data-hint="For this vehicle meaning, place it after got on." data-fix="Maya got on it at the main entrance.">choose</button> at the main entrance.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The shuttle reached the workshop, and <span class="ex">it</span> still replaces the shuttle: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="got off it" data-hint="For leaving the vehicle, place it after got off." data-fix="Maya got off it at the workshop.">choose</button> at the workshop.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Let context select the meaning and register</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>One written chunk can have more than one meaning and sometimes more than one object pattern. Store a separate example for each meaning you need.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>chunk</th><th>meaning 1</th><th>meaning 2</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody><tr><td><span class="ex">pick up</span></td><td><span class="ex">pick the box up</span>: lift it</td><td><span class="ex">pick up the box at the desk</span>: collect it</td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">take off</span></td><td><span class="ex">take your coat off</span>: remove it</td><td><span class="ex">the plane took off</span>: left the ground</td></tr><tr><td><span class="ex">turn down</span></td><td><span class="ex">turn the music down</span>: reduce the level</td><td><span class="ex">turn the invitation down</span>: reject it</td></tr></tbody>
  </table>
  <p>If context is unclear, ask: <span class="ex">Do you mean lift the box or collect it from the desk?</span> Clarifying the intended meaning is better than guessing from the particle.</p>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Register: common does not mean careless</h3>
    <p>Many phrasal verbs are neutral in everyday U.S. English: <span class="ex">fill out a form, turn off the projector, call someone back</span>. A more official text may use <span class="ex">complete the form, power off the equipment, return the call</span>. <span class="ex">Put off</span> and <span class="ex">postpone</span> can both mean move something to a later time, but tone and collocation differ. Do not replace every phrasal verb mechanically; follow the context and the wording of an official instruction.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: stress meaning, not a memorized particle rule</h3>
    <p>The particle often carries a clear result beat in separable chunks: <span class="ex">turn it <strong>OFF</strong> · pick them <strong>UP</strong> · write it <strong>DOWN</strong></span>. In fixed combinations, the preposition may be lighter: <span class="ex"><strong>LOOK</strong> for it · <strong>WAIT</strong> for her · <strong>LISTEN</strong> to it</span>. Actual stress changes when the speaker corrects or contrasts information.</p>
    <p>Link short pronouns into the chunk: <span class="ex">turn_it_OFF · pick_them_UP · write_it_DOWN · look_for_it</span>. Keep the final consonants audible so <span class="ex">pick</span> does not become <span class="ex">pig</span> and <span class="ex">back</span> does not become <span class="ex">bag</span>.</p>
    <p>In connected U.S. speech, <span class="ex">Can you pick it up?</span> may begin /kənjə/, and <span class="ex">Did you write it down?</span> may begin /dɪdʒə/. Stress the correction: <span class="ex">Turn it <strong>OFF</strong>, not <strong>DOWN</strong>.</span></p>
  </div>

